Milwaukee's climate is influenced to a considerable extent by Lake Michigan. This is especially true in the spring, summer, and fall months when the temperature of the lake water differs considerably from the air temperature. During the spring and early summer a shift of wind from a westerly to an easterly direction frequently causes a sudden 
10^{\circ}
 to 
15^{\circ}
 drop in temperatures. In the autumn, the relatively warm water of the lake prevents night-time temperatures from falling as low as they do a few miles inland from the shorelines.
Lake Breeze fronts reach the station perhaps 2 or 3 times a month in the spring, summer and fall season.
As shown from a portion of the local climatological data recorded by the National Neather Service Office at General Mitchell Field the study area experiences a yearly average temperature of about 
46^{\circ}
, with monthly temperatures ranging from 
11^{\circ}
 in January to 
80^{\circ}
 in July. See figure 11. The average annual precipitation is 29 inches with snowfall averaging about 44 inches. Prevailing winds are southwesterly during the months of July through October, northwesterly from November through March, and northeasterly from April through May. Monthly wind speeds range from about 10 to 13 mph with an annual average of 12 mph. Possible sunshine has averaged about 56 percent for the year with possible sunshine ranging from 38 percent in December to 70 percent in July.
